    "narrative": "For the August 20th slate, the Austin Waves offense centers around two marquee matchups. **Gunnar Henderson** leads the way with a 74.74 efficiency score, defying a difficult date with Gerrit Cole by leveraging a massive +16% park factor and the inherent benefits of a favorable platoon split. Following closely, **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (73.66) and **Sam Antonacci** (67.67) capitalize on secondary matchups against Kyle Bradish and Grant Holmes, respectively. **Antonacci** remains a priority play given our focus on stolen base efficiency, as his matchup against the weaker pitching of Holmes provides the necessary environment to maximize his speed profile. **Heliot Ramos** (64.74) and **Drake Baldwin** (64.68) round out our high-floor starters, with both benefiting from favorable SP-xERA figures north of 4.30.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Nasim Nunez**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a score of 36.77. While **Nunez** remains a vital asset for his elite sprint speed and base-stealing upside, the matchup against Jacob deGrom proved too daunting; the heavy penalty for facing such an elite arm, combined with the disadvantage of a #9 lineup slot, pushed him below our threshold. We are also managing significant roster turbulence elsewhere, as **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**, **Kyle Tucker**, and **Luis Robert Jr.** remain sidelined, leaving us to rely on **Dominic Smith** and **Coby Mayo** to fill the gaps. While **Smith** (47.65) and **Mayo** (45.87) sit in the marginal category, they are our best available options to navigate the current injury climate, which continues to impact our depth players like **Jackson Merrill**, **Andy Pages**, **Jordan Westburg**, **Josh Jung**, **Curtis Mead**, **Liam Hicks**, and **Luis Lara**. All managers should note that these scores assume projected batting orders; once official lineups drop, any significant drop in batting position for **Drake Baldwin** or **Jacob Wilson** could trigger a re-evaluation of our efficiency floor.",
